Note = {12th Mexican Conference on Pattern Recognition (MCPR), Morelia, MEXICO,
   JUN 24-27, 2020},
Organization = {Univ Michoacana San Nicolas Hidalgo, Fac Ingn Electrica; Univ Michoacana
   San Nicolas Hidalgo, Fac Ciencias Fisico Matematicas; Natl Univ
   Astrophys Opt Elect Mexio, Comp Sci Dept; Mexican Assoc Comp Vision
   Neurocomputing \& Robot},
Abstract = {The increasing propagation of abusive language in social media is a
   major concern for supplier companies and governments because of its
   negative social impact. A large number of methods have been developed
   for its automatic identification, ranging from dictionary-based methods
   to sophisticated deep learning approaches. A common problem in all these
   methods is to distinguish the offensive use of swear words from their
   everyday and humorous usage. To tackle this particular issue we propose
   an attention-based neural network architecture that captures the word
   n-grams importance according to their context. The obtained results in
   four standard collections from Twitter and Facebook are encouraging,
   they outperform the F-1 scores from state-of-the-art methods and allow
   identifying a set of inherently offensive swear words, and others in
   which its interpretation depends on its context.},
