   Systems (AAMAS), Montreal, CANADA, MAY 13-17, 2019},
Organization = {Assoc Comp Machinery; Int Fdn Autonomous Agents \& MultiAgent Syst; NSF;
   Artificial Intelligence Journal; Tourisme Montreal; J P Morgan;
   DeepMind; ACM SIGAI; Concordia Univ},
Abstract = {The need for increased maritime security has prompted research focus on
   intent recognition solutions for the naval domain. We consider the
   problem of early classification of the hostile behavior of agents in a
   dynamic maritime domain and propose a solution using multinomial Hidden
   Markov Models (HMMs). To enable early detection of hostile behaviors,
   the proposed approach encodes as observable symbols the rate of change
   (instead of static values) for parameters relevant to the task. We
   discuss our implementation of a one-versus-all intent classifier using
   multinomial HMMs and present the results of our system on three types of
   hostile behaviors (ram, herd, block) and a benign behavior.},
